From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: W4HO <87742609+W4HO-ham@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 14:24:58 -0647
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: brcmfmac: pcie: reset the BCM4383 with an FLR too

The BCM4383 (Pixel 9a) shares its chip with Bluetooth. With Bluetooth
up, so with BT_REG_ON high, an unbind and rebind of the PCIe host (which
drops and raises WL_REG_ON) left brcmfmac unable to start the chip
again: it recognised the chip and downloaded the firmware, then failed
with "FW failed to initialize". With hci_uart unloaded first, the same
rebind brought wlan0 back.

Broadcom's driver never resets this chip with the chipcommon watchdog.
bcmdhd sets cc_wd_reset false, so dhdpcie_dongle_reset() resets the
WLAN function with an FLR (dhd_bus_perform_flr()), with no check on
Bluetooth: "For buscorerev = 66 and after, F0 FLR should be done
independent from F1. So don't need BT quiesce" (dhd_pcie.c,
google-modules/wlan/bcm4383). The chip advertises FLReset+.

Reset it with the FLR the BCM4389 already uses, falling back to the
watchdog if the FLR is refused or doesn't complete.

Tested on a Pixel 9a (7.3.0-rc6-omamobile-push1-gd60d217db5c6): three
unbinds and rebinds of the PCIe host with Bluetooth powered each brought
wlan0 back, the link at Gen2 x1 every time, with hci0 still powered
afterwards. Without this commit the same test failed twice out of two
with "FW failed to initialize".

Signed-off-by: W4HO <87742609+W4HO-ham@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 .../broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/pcie.c        | 22 +++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/pcie.c b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/pcie.c
index 969410ee3..f53ded28b 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/pcie.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/pcie.c
@@ -948,6 +948,19 @@ static void brcmf_pcie_set_bar_coherent(struct brcmf_pciedev_info *devinfo)
  */
 #define BRCMF_PCIE_SSCTRL_SSRESET_STATUS	BIT(13)
 
+/*
+ * Chips Broadcom's driver resets with a function 0 FLR rather than the
+ * chipcommon watchdog (bcmdhd dhdpcie_dongle_reset(), cc_wd_reset false).
+ * The BCM4383 shares the chip with its Bluetooth: while BT_REG_ON holds the
+ * chip up, the watchdog reset leaves the WLAN cores in a state where the
+ * downloaded firmware never comes up, and an F0 FLR leaves Bluetooth alone.
+ */
+static bool brcmf_pcie_reset_by_flr(struct brcmf_pciedev_info *devinfo)
+{
+	return brcmf_pcie_is_4389_family(devinfo) ||
+	       devinfo->pdev->device == BRCM_PCIE_4383_DEVICE_ID;
+}
+
 static int brcmf_pcie_flr(struct brcmf_pciedev_info *devinfo)
 {
 	struct pci_dev *pdev = devinfo->pdev;
@@ -1014,12 +1027,13 @@ static void brcmf_pcie_reset_device(struct brcmf_pciedev_info *devinfo)
 			       val);
 
 	/* Watchdog reset, with all power domains up as it starts. The
-	 * BCM4389 is reset the way Broadcom's driver resets it, with an
-	 * FLR: after a chipcommon watchdog reset its ARM does not boot a
-	 * second firmware download.
+	 * BCM4389 and BCM4383 are reset the way Broadcom's driver resets
+	 * them, with an FLR: after a chipcommon watchdog reset the BCM4389's
+	 * ARM does not boot a second firmware download, and the BCM4383's
+	 * doesn't while its Bluetooth is on.
 	 */
 	brcmf_pcie_srpwr_request(devinfo);
-	if (!brcmf_pcie_is_4389_family(devinfo) || brcmf_pcie_flr(devinfo)) {
+	if (!brcmf_pcie_reset_by_flr(devinfo) || brcmf_pcie_flr(devinfo)) {
 		brcmf_pcie_select_core(devinfo, BCMA_CORE_CHIPCOMMON);
 		WRITECC32(devinfo, watchdog, 4);
 		msleep(100);
